Relay call-set allowlist - the relay is deny-by-default, shipped LOG-ONLY


1. SUMMARY

   relay.php used to dispatch ANY method of ANY registered class, with arguments
   taken from the request. Its only gate was "is someone logged in": the method
   string went into lib/loader.php's CallMethod(), which resolves the namespace to
   a file, instantiates the class and calls the method. So every quoting omission
   anywhere in the tree was remotely reachable by anyone holding a session, and
   nothing in the request distinguished a method the client actually calls from a
   method that merely happens to exist.

   The relay now consults an allowlist of relay method strings before it
   dispatches (Relay::handle_request(), after the LoggedIn guard, so both
   providers - Json and Xmlrpc - inherit it). A method that is not named in the
   list is a MISS. The list lives in data/config/relay-allowlist.php; the matcher
   is lib/org/freemedsoftware/core/Relay_Allowlist.class.php.

   The check runs for EVERY method, including the org.freemedsoftware.public.*
   namespace. That namespace still skips the login guard (relay.php's only
   validation is the provider name, and the login flow runs before there is a
   session), but it no longer skips this.

   The check runs for INVERSIONS of the relay too. A relay method that
   re-dispatches a method named by its own caller is the same door with a second
   handle: the outer method string is checked once, and the inner one would never
   meet the allowlist. There are three relay-reachable re-dispatchers in this
   tree, and they name their inner call on TWO different axes:

     * the METHOD axis - the method string itself is caller-supplied.
       org.freemedsoftware.api.UserInterface.Multicall is that shape (it is a
       listed method - a real client calls it - and it dispatches an array of
       {method, parameters} itself), so it now gates every inner call through the
       SAME decision point the relay uses (Relay_Allowlist::refuse(), with the
       INNER scope). In the log-only stage an inner miss is logged and still
       runs; under enforcement it is answered with INVALID_CALL in its own slot
       and the rest of the batch still runs.

     * the CLASS axis - the method name is a FIXED literal and the CLASS is
       caller-chosen. The wrappers in api/ModuleInterface.class.php
       (ModuleAddMethod, ModuleDeleteMethod, ModuleGetRecordMethod,
       ModuleGetRecordsMethod, ModuleModifyMethod, ModuleSupportPicklistMethod,
       EMRSupportPicklistMethod, ModuleRenderHtmlMethod, ModuleToTextMethod)
       dispatch one of eight fixed literals against the module class the caller
       passes, and the print wrappers (PrintToFax, PrintToPrinter, PrintToBrowser)
       dispatch the fixed literal RenderToPDF against the module class named by
       the patient_emr row they were pointed at. Listing the WRAPPER method
       therefore cannot constrain the class, so each wrapper resolves the
       concrete string it is about to dispatch -
       org.freemedsoftware.module.<Class>.<literal> - and gates THAT. See
       section 1a.

       api/FormTemplate.class.php:ProcessData is the third re-dispatcher: its
       names come from the XML template file it was pointed at, not from the
       request, and both of its axes (a core.* object method and a module.*
       method) are gated the same way.

   ANY OTHER re-dispatcher carrying a caller-supplied method name or a
   caller-chosen class belongs on that same entry point.


1a. THE CLASS AXIS, AND THE NEVER-ALLOW LIST

   The class axis is worth stating in full, because it is the axis a per-method
   allowlist cannot see:

     POST /relay.php/json/org.freemedsoftware.api.ModuleInterface.ModuleAddMethod
     param0=<ANY module class>   param1=<data>

   ModuleAddMethod is listed (a real client calls it) and it dispatches the fixed
   literal 'add' against the class in param0. Before fix round 2 that reached
   EVERY module class in the tree, including classes no pattern names. Each
   wrapper now gates org.freemedsoftware.module.<Class>.<literal>.

   WHAT THAT MEANS FOR ENABLING ENFORCEMENT. The measured exposure is large: over
   the 133 module class files, 866 (class, literal) pairs exist for the eight
   literals and 803 of them are NOT in the shipped list (903 / 840 if you count
   the print wrappers' RenderToPDF). So a wrapper call naming a module class
   whose literal is not in 'patterns' is REFUSED once 'enforce' => true - that is
   the point of the gate, and it is why the seed deliberately does NOT enumerate
   this axis. Run the log-only stage (section 3) and let the miss log tell you
   which of those pairs your site's workflows actually use; add those, not the
   866. The full enumeration, with each pair's verdict, is the served section
   "class axis" of tests/security/evidence/relay-callset.txt.

   NEVER-ALLOW NAMESPACES. 'never_allow' in the data file is a list of namespace
   prefixes that are refused EVEN IF 'patterns' names them, in BOTH stages, when
   they arrive as an INNER call with a caller-supplied method name. The shipped
   seed is 'org.freemedsoftware.core.'. This is not a new rule: the pre-2.8
   Multicall() carried

       if ( substr($v['method'], 0, 25) == 'org.freemedsoftware.core.' ) { ... return false; }

   and the literal 'org.freemedsoftware.core.' is 25 characters, so the
   comparison MATCHED and the guard refused every inner call in the `core`
   namespace outright - in both stages. Fix round 1 removed it as "dead code"
   (a false claim); fix round 2 reinstates it as data. Restoring it is what puts
   the shipped log-only stage back at parity with pre-2.8: without it, an inner
   core.* call proceeded where the old code refused it.

   THE OUTER PATH IS ASYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE. An OUTER call in a never-allow
   namespace is logged (LOG_WARNING) and NOT refused, so shipped outer behaviour
   is exactly what it was before this control existed. (Two core.* methods are
   genuinely reachable as outer calls and are in 'patterns': core.User.getName
   and core.User.setPassword.) Only the inner, caller-supplied-name path refuses.
   For the same reason, FormTemplate::ProcessData - whose names come from the
   template FILE, not the request - is gated with the OUTER scope: the log-only
   stage must never start refusing a call it served before the control existed.

   THIS CONTROL SHIPS IN LOG-ONLY MODE. 'enforce' => false. A MISS IS LOGGED AT
   LOG_WARNING AND THE CALL STILL EXECUTES. See section 2 - the reason is not
   timidity, it is that the call set in the list has never been exercised by a
   real client in this tree, and enforcing a call set no real client has used is
   how a security control becomes a production outage.

   This is NOT an authorization check. It answers "is this method part of the
   relay's call set", not "may this user call it". Per-method access control
   remains each module's job, and nothing here changes it.


2. WHY THE DEFAULT IS LOG-ONLY, AND WHAT IT COSTS

   The list was built from the best enumeration this tree can produce, and the
   enumeration is committed with per-pattern provenance as
   tests/security/evidence/relay-callset.txt. It is not the same thing as a real
   client smoke pass: there is no compiled GWT client in this checkout, so no
   browser session has ever exercised this call set. The tiers, in descending
   order of confidence:

     * live-probe - methods this mitigation has already driven against a running
       server (the four fixed SQL-injection sites' methods, plus the batch-B /
       batch-C / 2.6f probe sets). Real, measured, client-shaped calls.
     * access-log - methods the stack's Apache access log shows being requested.
       The brief warned that compiled JS may call methods which never appear in
       source, and this is the source that catches exactly that: it is how
       PaymentModule.GetLedger (34 requests), Vitals.RenderHtmlView (19) and
       Vitals.locked (21) were found. Still probe traffic, not a client session -
       the capture and its time window are in
       tests/security/evidence/relay-accesslog.txt.
     * gwtphpmap - lib/org/freemedsoftware/gwt/**/*.gwtphpmap.inc.php, the
       shipped GWT service map's client-to-relay table. The strongest
       machine-readable evidence of what the compiled client asks for.
     * public-namespace - the public/* call sites in the client and the
       templates, plus the remaining public methods of those same classes,
       because relay.php's login-guard exemption means a missing entry breaks the
       install or login flow itself.
     * source-mine - literals resolved out of the client and UI trees, kept only
       where the class file exists and the method is public. This is the tier
       most likely to be incomplete for a real session.

   With 'enforce' => false all of that is a measurement rather than a bet: an
   entry this enumeration missed is logged and runs, and the log is the list of
   what to add before turning enforcement on.


3. THE SWITCH, AND HOW TO TURN ENFORCEMENT ON

   data/config/relay-allowlist.php, 'enforce':

       false   LOG-ONLY. A miss is logged at LOG_WARNING with the method, the
               remote address and whether it was refused; the call executes.
               This is the shipped value.
       true    ENFORCING. A miss is logged the same way and answered with
               INVALID_CALL, and the method does not run.

   ...and 'never_allow', which is a SEPARATE list and is NOT governed by
   'enforce':

       A namespace prefix in 'never_allow' is refused in BOTH stages when it
       arrives as an INNER call with a caller-supplied method name, even if
       'patterns' names it. The shipped seed is 'org.freemedsoftware.core.',
       which is the pre-2.8 Multicall() refusal (section 1a). On the OUTER path
       the same namespace is logged at LOG_WARNING and NOT refused, so shipped
       outer behaviour is unchanged. Two lines, two `grep`s:

           Relay: method '...' is not in the relay allowlist (remote=..., ...)
           Relay: method '...' is in a NEVER-ALLOW namespace 'org.freemedsoftware.core.'; ...

       Removing a never-allow entry is a WIDENING of the call set and belongs in
       the same review as removing a check; an entry that is malformed or so
       broad it would refuse every inner call (e.g. 'org.freemedsoftware.') is
       REJECTED at load with a LOG_WARNING and is not honoured.

   Procedure - do not skip to step 4:

     1. Deploy with 'enforce' => false (as shipped).
     2. Verify by hand that the control is live: a method NOT in the list must
        log. Any session, any unlisted method, e.g.
            POST /relay.php/json/org.freemedsoftware.api.UserInterface.getRel
        prints to syslog
            Relay: method '...' is not in the relay allowlist
            (remote=<ip>, LOG-ONLY, call still executed)
        If nothing is logged, the control is not running and step 4 would be
        theatre. (Set the test up the other way round if the site is already
        enforcing: the line then says "refused (INVALID_CALL)" and the body is
        the string INVALID_CALL.)
     3. Use the system normally on the real compiled client - every screen,
        report, print, export and the installer - for long enough to cover this
        site's workflows, and watch for misses:
            grep "Relay: method" /var/log/syslog
        Add each method named there to 'patterns'. Copy the string from the log
        line; matching is case-insensitive for the method name, so its case does
        not have to match the class's spelling.
     4. When the log is quiet, set 'enforce' => true.

   IMPORTANT - A CONFIG EDIT IS NOT INSTANT, AND THE LAG IS PER WORKER. PHP
   caches the data file through OPcache: with the default opcache.revalidate_freq
   = 2 a worker can still serve the previous value for a couple of seconds after
   the file changes, and TWO WORKERS CAN DISAGREE about which value is current.
   So a SINGLE PROBE IS NOT EVIDENCE, in either direction - it is how both a
   false "the control is broken" and a false PASS were produced while this
   control was being verified. Reload PHP (or wait past opcache.revalidate_freq
   on every worker) and probe MORE THAN ONCE before believing that the running
   server agrees with the file you just edited, and before trusting either
   answer.

   IMPORTANT - A MISSING OR UNPARSEABLE DATA FILE DOES NOT ENFORCE. If
   data/config/relay-allowlist.php is unreadable, or does not return an array,
   or cannot be parsed at all (a typo while hand-editing `patterns` - the editing
   this document instructs - raises a ParseError that `@include` does not
   suppress), the relay logs a warning and falls back to log-only rather than
   refusing every call. A parse failure is logged at LOG_ERR because it is a
   deployment mistake, not an expected state. That is the outage-safe direction
   for a control whose default is log-only, but it means that once a site
   enforces, the file belongs in version control and in the backup.

   THE MISS LINE CARRIES THE METHOD, THE REMOTE ADDRESS AND THE STAGE - AND
   NOTHING ELSE. There is no user and no site identifier in it. On a
   reverse-proxied host every client looks like the proxy, and on a multi-tenant
   host one syslog cannot say whose miss it was: if that distinction matters for
   your deployment, carry it in the log transport (a per-site syslog tag) or
   record the forwarding header at the proxy, rather than expecting it in this
   line.

   KEEP THE LIST HONEST. If a call is refused that should have been allowed, add
   a pattern. Do not remove the check, and do not widen a pattern to the whole
   tree. A trailing `*` is supported and is a real widening; it can be written at
   three widths, and the widest one is honoured:

       org.freemedsoftware.module.SomeModule.*       a CLASS
           - every method of that one class
       org.freemedsoftware.module.SomeModule.Get*    a PREFIX
           - every method name beginning `Get`
       org.freemedsoftware.module.*                  a NAMESPACE
           - EVERY method of EVERY class under that namespace

   The NAMESPACE form is ACCEPTED - it is the brief's own example of a `*`
   pattern and it is narrower than a whole-tree rule - but it is NEVER ACCEPTED
   IN SILENCE: the load logs it at LOG_WARNING and names how many method
   declarations it covers, because a wildcard accepted with no log line cannot be
   told apart from the control being switched off. A pattern BROADER than those -
   a bare `*`, org.freemedsoftware.*, org.freemedsoftware, or a bare namespace
   with no trailing `*` such as org.freemedsoftware.api - is REJECTED at load
   with a LOG_WARNING and does not take effect, as is a `*` anywhere but the end
   of a pattern (a wildcard the matcher does not implement; honouring it
   literally would leave the operator believing a rule was in force that matches
   nothing), and as is an exact pattern that is not exactly
   org.freemedsoftware.<ns>.<Class>.<Method> (a deeper spelling names no relay
   method and would match nothing).

   EXTENDING IT: `patterns` is a list of exact relay method strings, or the same
   with a trailing `*` to match any method string with that prefix
   ('org.freemedsoftware.module.SomeModule.*'). The shipped seed uses no `*`
   patterns: it is 323 resolved method strings. Matching is literal - the dots
   are dots, not regular-expression wildcards - and case-insensitive, mirroring
   PHP's own method dispatch, so a client that writes `Picklist` for a class that
   declares `picklist` is not refused.

   DEPLOYMENTS THAT ADD THEIR OWN MODULES should add that module's methods to
   this file in the same commit as the module. The controlled extension point is
   this file; the alternative is patching Relay.class.php, which is what the data
   file exists to avoid.


4. RELEASE NOTE

   Release note - relay call-set allowlist

   The JSON and XML-RPC relays no longer dispatch arbitrary methods. relay.php
   would call any method of any registered class with request-supplied
   arguments, gated only on being logged in, so a quoting mistake anywhere in the
   tree was remotely reachable. The relay now checks the requested method against
   an allowlist (data/config/relay-allowlist.php) before dispatching.

   The control ships in log-only mode: a method that is not in the list is
   logged at LOG_WARNING and still runs, so this release cannot break a working
   UI. Operators who want the control enforced should run the enable procedure in
   section 3 - the log-only period is what produces the list of methods to add.
   No configuration is required to keep the previous behaviour, and no
   database change is involved: the list is a file in the installation.

   Two changes in this release are worth a line of their own, because they
   change what the control covers rather than only where it runs:

     * The fixed-literal wrappers in api/ModuleInterface.class.php (add, del,
       GetRecord, GetRecords, mod, picklist, RenderHtmlView, to_text, and the
       print wrappers' RenderToPDF) used to reach ANY module class the caller
       named. They now gate the concrete method string they dispatch, so
       enforcement covers the class a caller chooses and not only the method.
     * The inner-call refusal of the `core` namespace - which Multicall() carried
       before this control existed and which an earlier revision of this change
       removed - is back, now as a documented 'never_allow' entry in the data
       file. Next to it, calls in that namespace on the OUTER path are logged and
       not refused, so no outer call that worked before is refused now.
     * api/PatientInterface.class.php::MoveEmrAttachments gates the module hook it
       dispatches (`additional_move`) on the class the caller's chosen record
       resolves to, and it does so BEFORE THE FIRST WRITE of the loop. A refusal
       under enforcement is therefore ATOMIC for that attachment: nothing is
       written, no annotation is moved and the hook does not run, and the refusal
       is reported through the method's own return value. This matters because the
       gate sat after those writes in an earlier revision of this change, which
       would have left a refused call with the record already moved. In the
       shipped log-only stage nothing changes: the miss is logged and the call
       runs, so the stage measures which (module class, literal) pairs a site
       actually uses before anyone enforces them.


